---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const relatedLinks = [];
const _body = "<p>Constraint validation covers two different things in Traverse, and it matters which is which: one is enforced by the runtime today, the other is currently documentation for reviewers.</p>\n\n      <h2>Sandbox constraints</h2>\n      <p>A contract's <span class=\"code-inline\">execution.constraints</span> block declares three fields, each a fixed string value rather than a boolean:</p>\n      <div class=\"code-block\">\"constraints\": {\n  \"host_api_access\": \"none\",       // or \"exception_required\"\n  \"network_access\": \"forbidden\",  // or \"required\"\n  \"filesystem_access\": \"none\"     // or \"sandbox_only\"\n}</div>\n      <p>As of v0.1 this is mostly a declaration for human and tooling review, not something the runtime enforces at execution time — it does not inspect WASM imports, apply a network sandbox, or pre-open directories based on these values. The one value it does check structurally is <span class=\"code-inline\">host_api_access: exception_required</span>: bundle validation rejects a contract that sets it without at least one entry in <span class=\"code-inline\">provenance.exception_refs</span>.</p>\n\n      <p><strong>Preconditions</strong> and <strong>postconditions</strong> are a contract's declared assertions about the input or output. Each is just an id and a human-readable description — there's no expression language — and the runtime does not evaluate them before or after execution in v0.x. They're documentation for reviewers and spec coverage, not an enforcement mechanism.</p>\n\n      <div class=\"code-block\">\"preconditions\": [\n  { \"id\": \"input-provided\", \"description\": \"A required field is present.\" }\n]</div>\n\n      <h2>What the runtime actually checks before execution</h2>\n      <p>Two things gate execution for real: the caller's input is validated against the contract's <span class=\"code-inline\">input_schema</span> (a mismatch returns <span class=\"code-inline\">request_invalid</span>), and candidate selection filters out capabilities that are out of scope, not runnable for the requested placement, or whose lifecycle marks them ineligible — the trace records each rejected candidate with a reason such as <span class=\"code-inline\">not_runnable_locally</span> or <span class=\"code-inline\">lifecycle_not_runnable</span>. If nothing remains, the run ends in <span class=\"code-inline\">capability_not_found</span>. After execution, the output is checked against <span class=\"code-inline\">output_schema</span>; a mismatch returns <span class=\"code-inline\">output_validation_failed</span>.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why this distinction matters</h2>\n      <p>If you're counting on a contract's declared sandbox constraints or preconditions to stop a bad call by themselves, they won't yet — today those fields are review-time documentation. Put anything that must actually be enforced into the input/output JSON Schema itself, or into the WASM implementation's own logic.</p>";
const _jsonLd = "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How does constraint validation work in Traverse?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"A contract declares sandbox constraints (host_api_access, network_access, filesystem_access) and preconditions/postconditions, but as of v0.1 those are mostly documentation for reviewers rather than runtime-enforced gates. What the runtime does enforce before execution is input-schema validation and candidate selection filtering, and output-schema validation after.\"}}]}";
---
<QuestionLayout
  title={"How does constraint validation work in Traverse?"}
  description={"A Traverse contract declares sandbox constraints and preconditions/postconditions, but as of v0.1 those are mostly review-time documentation. What the runtime actually enforces before a WASM binary runs is input-schema validation and candidate selection filtering."}
  canonical={"https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-does-constraint-validation-work.html"}
  category={"Technical Architecture"}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={_jsonLd}
>
  <Fragment set:html={_body} />
</QuestionLayout>
